<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hieronymus Wolf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1516">1516</a>–<a href="/years/1580">1580</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hieronymus Wolf was a German humanist scholar and librarian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> who edited and translated Greek authors. His Corpus Historiae Byzantinae of 1557, a collection of medieval Greek historians, did much to popularise the term 'Byzantine' for the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, whose people had called themselves Romans. He is often regarded as a founder of Byzantine studies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hieronymus-wolf.jpg" alt="Hieronymus Wolf" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Colossus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hieronymus_wolf2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hieronymus Wolf was born at Oettingen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, one of nine children of an impoverished office clerk. He was formally educated as an attorney but worked for years as a scribe. He studied for a time at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>, where he was impressed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philipp Melanchthon</a> and exposed to Lutheran teaching, and he is said to have saved money from his small income to buy a Latin-Greek dictionary and taught himself Greek. His German translation of the speeches of Demosthenes, published in 1549 by the house of Oporinus, made his name known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugger-family">Fugger family</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Wolf became secretary and librarian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, where he studied and translated many ancient and medieval Greek authors and made them accessible to German academics. The library became known for its contents, among them 100 Greek manuscripts transferred from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and under Wolf and others it became a respected research centre. He was later appointed the first rector of the Gelehrtenschule in the former Carmelite cloister of St Anne, a Protestant college set up to counterbalance the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> college created at about the same time. He was sick throughout his life and never married, and was reputed an outstanding teacher though secluded and egocentric. He died aged 64.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Until his time no distinction had been made between ancient and medieval Greek works, and the latter were overshadowed by interest in the classics. Wolf turned to the history that had led to the Ottoman conquest of much of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, which he lived to see reach the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. In 1557 he published the Corpus Historiae Byzantinae, a collection of Byzantine sources rather than a comprehensive history. He is credited as the first to call the Eastern Roman Empire Byzantine, a term that spread among western European scholars. In the 17th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> prompted a larger edition in 34 volumes with Greek text and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation, built on Wolf's Corpus, which established the term in historical studies.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hieronymus Wolf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Through it the name of the small Megarian colony passed to an entire civilisation, for early modern scholars, notably <b>Hieronymus Wolf</b> in the sixteenth century, called the medieval Eastern Roman state the Byzantine Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Oettingen in Bayern, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.95,10.58&amp;year=1557" aria-label="Oettingen in Bayern on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4795 455 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hieronymus Wolf"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5245 725h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5245 725h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.95,10.58&amp;year=1557">What was here in 1557? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
